WEBVTT

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:12.290
<v Speaker 1>Hi, this is Brian David Marshall from magicgathering.com. I'm here at the 2005 Magic the Gathering World Championships, where we've just completed the induction ceremonies for the inaugural class of the Pro Tour Hall of Fame.</v>

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.350
<v Speaker 1>The first inductee into the Hall of Fame was Oli Rating.</v>

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:47.000
<v Speaker 1>Well, it's used to being the first. He's been doing it his whole career. He was the first European to win a Pro Tour, something he accomplished in his very first attempt at the age of 16 during Pro Tour Columbus. He went on to become the first player of the year and also won the first Magic Invitational, which was commemorated with the card Sylvan Safekeeper. Most importantly, Olay has his first was the first player to top 8 five times in his career, something that only took him 15 Pro Tours to accomplish. Our second inductee was Alan Comer.</v>

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.890
<v Speaker 1>Allen has accomplished many things in this game.</v>

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:57.720
<v Speaker 1>After his first top 8 at the second Pro Tour, LA, Allen went on to top 8 four more times and made two trips to the finals.</v>

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:13.530
<v Speaker 1>Alan is widely regarded as one of the game's most important deck designer. The ripples of his creations are still felt today. Decks like the Mono Blue Turbo Xerox, the original Reanimator, and the always popular Miracle Grow have made lasting impressions on the Magic community.</v>

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:30.910
<v Speaker 1>The only thing that overshadows Alan's accomplishments in the game are the sportsmanship he brought to the game and the pure joy he always took from it. The third inductee into the Hall of Fame was Tomi Hobby. During the opening remarks, Chris Galvin mentioned that Rich Richard Garfield's dream</v>

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.440
<v Speaker 1>was to have magic become recognized as an intellectual sport.</v>

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:44.200
<v Speaker 1>Tony was a key figure in realizing that dream when he became the first player to win 2 Pro Tours. He won the second Pro Tour LA and also won Pro Tour Rome.</v>

00:01:45.330 --> 00:02:02.980
<v Speaker 1>The first of Tommy's four top aides came at Worlds in 1996. On the road to that world, Tommy Stasis Deck dominated Finnish nationals and completely warped the meta game for US nationals. And right up until Worlds, he was a central figure in spreading magic around the globe.</v>

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.090
<v Speaker 1>The penultimate inductee</v>

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.570
<v Speaker 1>for the inaugural Hall of Fame class was Darwin Castle.</v>

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:21.570
<v Speaker 1>Darwin began playing on the Pro Tour with the very first event and remained a fixture on the tour from his first top aide at the first Pro Tour LA right through his stunning 8th Pro Tour Top 8 at Pro Tour Venice.</v>

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.500
<v Speaker 1>Along the way, Darwin became one of the first players to take up the professional Magic player lifestyle.</v>

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:45.570
<v Speaker 1>In terms of Pro Tour points, he's the third winningest player in the games history and he's among a small group of players who have won a GP, a PT and an Invitational. His Pro Tour win came at the First Team Pro Tour alongside Rob Doherty and Dave Humphreys as Team Your Move games.</v>

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.610
<v Speaker 1>His Invitational win was commemorated with Avalanche riders.</v>

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.260
<v Speaker 1>Our final inductee needed little introduction.</v>

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.790
<v Speaker 1>Known simply as Johnny Magic John Finkel,</v>

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.520
<v Speaker 1>he's always been one of the most popular and most successful Magic players in the history of the game.</v>

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:11.850
<v Speaker 1>His resume is staggering. 11 Pro Tour top aides that don't even take into account 2 Junior Pro Tour top aides during the first year on tour.</v>

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:23.640
<v Speaker 1>John finished 9th in his first Grown Up tournament, World 1996 and would go on to become the Player of the Year in 1998 when he made three top aides including his win at Pro Tour New York.</v>

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:33.530
<v Speaker 1>In the year 2000, John had a remarkable run that saw him win US Nationals, the World Championships, and lead the US team to victory in the team competitions.</v>

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.930
<v Speaker 1>Somewhere along the line, he also found time to win the Magic Invitational and was immortalized as this Shadowmage Infiltrator.</v>

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:58.320
<v Speaker 1>John has won nearly $300,000 during his career in Magic, which is the second highest total in the games history. All of the players who were inducted into the Hall of Fame this morning support Richard Garfield's claim that Magic is an intellectual sport that requires great skill to play.</v>

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.540
<v Speaker 1>John, is the closing argument in that claim.</v>

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.070
<v Speaker 1>There you have it,</v>

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.050
<v Speaker 1>the inaugural class for the Pro Tour Hall of Fame of 2005.</v>

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:09.560
<v Speaker 1>Oh, they wrote</v>

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:11.420
<v Speaker 1>Alan Comer.</v>

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.120
<v Speaker 1>Tommy Hobby,</v>

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:14.850
<v Speaker 1>Darwin Castle</v>

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.620
<v Speaker 1>and John Finkel.</v>

